// What is left of the standard collections over an element that owns something.
//
// Three classes stand behind `Vector<T>`, and all three used to move elements
// by reading a slot and storing what they read -- and a handle read out of a
// slot is a second handle to one object. `Vector` and `VectorIterator` say the
// weaker thing now: a snapshot is `sub T[]`, `first`, `last` and `toArray`
// hand back the subscriber form, and at `T = int32` none of those qualifiers
// are there at all. `Vector<Cell*>` is an ordinary vector --
// tests/std-vector-owner-elements.abs runs one.
//
// `VectorBuilder` is the one that cannot be fixed by saying something weaker.
// It is a staging owner: `add(T)` gives it an element and `finish()` hands
// every element to a new `Vector<T>` that owns them. Seeded from a live
// vector, its first act is to duplicate what that vector still holds -- which
// is what a snapshot means, and what an owning element cannot be. Borrowing
// instead does not help: `finish()` would then give a vector of owners handles
// it never owned.
//
// What changed is where that is paid for. `builder()` used to be a method, and
// a method's return type is instantiated with the class, so `Vector<Cell*>`
// could not exist at all -- the builder was reached without being called. It
// is an extension now, instantiated where it is called, so the refusal below
// belongs to the line that asks for a builder rather than to every program
// that holds a vector.
//
// `Vector<int32>` is unaffected, and the rest of the suite uses exactly that.

import "../std/collections/vector.abs";

class Cell {
    public int32 value;
    public Cell(int32 initial) { value = initial; }
}

int32 main() {
    Vector<int32>* counted = new Vector<int32>();
    counted.push(1);
    delete counted;

    // An ordinary vector of owners. Nothing here is refused.
    Vector<Cell*>* owning = new Vector<Cell*>();
    owning.push(new Cell(1));
    sub Cell* borrowed = owning[0];

    // Asking it for a builder is what is refused, and this is the line that
    // asks.
    VectorBuilder<Cell*>* staged = owning.builder();

    delete owning;
    return borrowed.value;
}
